# DESCRIPTION

Section 1 holds the commands a person runs: rc(1), the shell, and the programs it starts, such as ls(1) and cat(1), and the tools on the dev machine, marked `host` in their headers, such as `vx` and its commands.

A command's SYNOPSIS is in Plan 9's notation.
The command and its flags are literal; every other word is a parameter, `[ ]` encloses what is optional, and `...` follows what may repeat.
Its usage message is the same line.

The programs a running system has are the files of its `/bin`, which a manifest binds from the boot image's `bin/`.
A program that serves a tree is documented in section 4, a driver in section 3, and one an administrator runs in section 8.

## How the manual is laid out

| Section | Holds |
| 1 | commands |
| 2 | Sys, the syscalls and the C library |
| 3 | devices and their drivers |
| 4 | services and the trees they serve |
| 5 | protocols |
| 6 | file formats |
| 7 | concepts |
| 8 | administration |

A page is found by any name it documents, and a name followed by a section in parentheses, such as guide(6), is a link to the page.
The pages are written in guide(6) and read with vx-man(1).

